# Motivation

Because lightningcss fails to parse the tailwindcss  v3.4.17 output, I can't use `cargo leptos serve`.

The proposed solution in this PR is to use the 'raw' input CSS without parsing or minification as a fallback.

`cargo leptos serve -vv` shows this now:

<img width="1872" height="376" alt="image" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/f0a24b08-eee2-4674-a404-5ea97f759cdb" />



https://github.com/leptos-rs/cargo-leptos/issues/599

This fallback concept is also found in **trunk**. A huge difference: **trunk does not minify the tailwindcss**, as attempted here in `cargo leptos`. In trunk it calls the **tailwindcss with -m** on release builds, which minimizes it already. Thus in trunk, it uses the CSS artifact without any parsing, I checked it, it is the same CSS code and same parser `lightningcss` in both tools.

# cargo-leptos

    cargo leptos serve --release
    warning: `frontend` (lib) generated 22 warnings
    error: could not compile `frontend` (lib) due to 1 previous error; 22 warnings emitted
        Tailwind finished tailwindcss --input style/style.css --config tailwind.config.js --output /home/nixos/planicus/target/tmp/tailwind.css --minify

So we could be faster when we don't minify an already minified target.

/home/nixos/cargo-leptos/src/compile/tailwind.rs

```rust
pub async fn tailwind_process(
    proj: &Project,
    cmd: &str,
    tw_conf: &TailwindConfig,
) -> Result<(String, Command)> {
    let tailwind = Exe::Tailwind.get().await.dot()?;

    let mut args = vec!["--input", tw_conf.input_file.as_str()];

    if let Some(config_file) = tw_conf.config_file.as_ref() {
        args.push("--config");
        args.push(config_file.as_str());
    }

    args.push("--output");
    args.push(tw_conf.tmp_file.as_str());

    if proj.release {
        // minify & optimize
        args.push("--minify");
    }

    let line = format!("{} {}", cmd, args.join(" "));
    let mut command = Command::new(tailwind);
    command.args(args);

    Ok((line, command))
}
```

