Real-time sync configuration was non-functional. Clicking "Save & Connect" with Firebase credentials had no effect because `initializeFirebase()` checked a hardcoded `firebaseConfig` object with placeholder values instead of reading saved localStorage values.

## Changes

**Core Fix**
- Extract `buildFirebaseConfig()` helper that reads from localStorage dynamically
- Update `initializeFirebase()` to use helper instead of hardcoded constant
- Remove redundant hardcoded `firebaseConfig` object

**Async Flow**
- Handle Firebase app deletion as Promise before reinitialization
- Remove arbitrary timeouts, use proper async/await flow
- Extract cleanup logic into helper functions to avoid duplication

**Validation & UX**
- Validate Firebase URL formats (database URL must include protocol, domain fields must exclude it)
- Add loading states with spinners on connection buttons
- Test database connectivity, not just initialization
- Update all status indicators via class selector (fix duplicate ID issue)

**Before:**
```javascript
const firebaseConfig = {
    apiKey: localStorage.getItem('firebaseApiKey') || "YOUR_API_KEY",
    // ... always checked "YOUR_API_KEY" placeholder
};

function initializeFirebase() {
    if (firebaseConfig.apiKey === "YOUR_API_KEY") {
        return false; // Always false even with saved config
    }
    // ...
}
```

**After:**
```javascript
function buildFirebaseConfig() {
    return {
        apiKey: localStorage.getItem('firebaseApiKey') || "YOUR_API_KEY",
        // ... dynamically built each call
    };
}

function initializeFirebase() {
    const currentConfig = buildFirebaseConfig();
    if (currentConfig.apiKey === "YOUR_API_KEY" || !currentConfig.apiKey) {
        return false; // Checks actual saved value
    }
    // ...
}
```

Real-time sync now connects properly when credentials are saved.

